README.md:85:**What that does to levonorgestrel.** Over the same days, levonorgestrel's clearance fell almost by half (CL/F 53.7 → 40.8 → 28.4 mL/h/kg), so exposure on day 21 was about twice day 1's. The minipill has no estrogen, so its SHBG stays put and norethindrone never builds up this way. This is the "two hormones affecting each other" half of the project's question.
README.md:97:**Limits.** The label also shows *unbound* levonorgestrel rising and its unbound clearance falling (2.79 → 1.57 L/h/kg), so the estrogen effect isn't binding alone. In the model, SHBG stands for the whole effect. The label's volume of distribution also shrinks over the cycle (Vz/F 2.66 → 1.43 L/kg), which the model doesn't include, so it underpredicts the week-3 peak.
README.md:123:The fitted model implies a clearance of about 230 L/day and a volume of about 1 L/kg, lower than the label's "about 600 L/day" and "about 4 L/kg". Those label figures come from separate studies and don't match the label's own concentration data, so I fit to the concentrations.
README.md:135:**What's missing without SHBG.** Taken daily for 21 days, the model without SHBG predicts a day-21 AUC of 35 ng·h/mL and a low point of 0.84 ng/mL just before the next pill. Alesse measured 68 ng·h/mL and 1.9 ng/mL. The day-21 AUC gap (×1.9) matches the label's fall in clearance from day 1 to day 21 (53.7 → 28.4 mL/h/kg, ×1.9), which is the SHBG effect.
README.md:210:- Parameters are averages from label studies. Real people vary, and published data show that body weight can roughly double levonorgestrel's half-life.
README.md:217:- **Body weight:** reproduce published differences in levonorgestrel kinetics between normal-BMI and obese users (Edelman et al., 2009), then see what the model predicts for the minipill, where no such data exist.
README.md:229:- Edelman, A. B., et al. (2009). [Impact of obesity on oral contraceptive pharmacokinetics and hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian activity](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2736633). *Contraception*, 80(2), 119–127.
