146: <main>
147:   <header>
148:     <div class="eyebrow">Norethindrone 0.35 mg vs. levonorgestrel 0.10 mg + ethinyl estradiol (Alesse)</div>
149:     <h1>Minipill vs. combined pill</h1>
150:     <p class="lede">Every model built so far, on the same charts: the minipill's norethindrone and the combined pill's levonorgestrel, each with its one- and two-compartment versions; how the combined pill's estrogen changes things through SHBG; a month of pills; and what a late pill does to each.</p>
151:   </header>
152: 
153:   <section class="plain" aria-labelledby="h-plain">
154:     <h2 id="h-plain">The short version</h2>
155:     <ul>
156:       <li>Both hormones peak an hour or two after the pill. <b>Norethindrone is half gone in about 8 hours; levonorgestrel in about a day and a half.</b></li>
157:       <li>The combined pill's estrogen also makes the body produce more <b>SHBG</b>, a protein that holds onto levonorgestrel and slows its removal. So its levels build up to about twice as high by week 3.</li>
158:       <li>A pill 3 hours late: the minipill dips <b>23%</b> below its usual low, the combined pill about <b>4%</b>. A whole pill missed: <b>88%</b> vs. <b>28%</b>.</li>
159:       <li>Each pill's CDC limit lands at a similar dip: the minipill at 3 hours late dips 23%; the combined pill with one pill missed (still under CDC's 48-hour limit) dips 28%. Past 48 hours (two pills missed) it dips 49%.</li>
160:     </ul>
161:   </section>
162: 
