58	197:       <h2 id="h-shbg">SHBG: why the combined pill builds up</h2>
59	198:       <p class="say"><span class="tag">In plain words</span>The combined pill has two hormones, and one helps the other stick around. Its estrogen tells the liver to make more of a carrier protein, SHBG. Levonorgestrel rides on SHBG in the blood, and while it's held there the liver clears it more slowly. The minipill has no estrogen, so this never happens.</p>
60	199:     </div>
61	200:     <div class="chain" aria-label="Cause and effect chain">Estrogen (ethinyl estradiol) → liver makes more <b>SHBG</b> → more levonorgestrel held in the blood → cleared more slowly → levels build up over the pack</div>
62	201:     <div class="explain">
63	202:       <div class="step"><h3>What SHBG is</h3><p>Sex hormone–binding globulin: a protein made by the liver that carries sex hormones in the blood. Levonorgestrel binds it strongly; about half of the levonorgestrel in blood is on SHBG.</p></div>
64	203:       <div class="step"><h3>What the estrogen does</h3><p>In the Alesse study, SHBG rose from 57 to 81 to 93 nmol/L on days 1, 6 and 21, about 60% higher by week 3. It levels off within about two weeks and drifts back down in the placebo week.</p></div>
65	204:       <div class="step"><h3>What that does to levonorgestrel</h3><p>Over the same days, levonorgestrel's clearance fell almost by half (53.7 → 28.4 mL/h/kg), so levels on day 21 were about twice day 1's. That stored-up hormone is what carries the combined pill through a missed pill.</p></div>
66	205:     </div>
