          <span class="inputs"><input type="number" id="ht-ft" inputmode="numeric" min="4" max="7" step="1" value="5" aria-label="Height, feet"> ft <input type="number" id="ht-in" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="11.9" step="0.5" value="5.5" aria-label="Height, inches"> in</span>
        </div>
        <label class="field"><span class="sr">Weight slider</span>
          <input type="range" id="wt-range" min="35" max="160" step="1" value="60" aria-label="Weight">
        </label>
      </div>
      <div class="body-stats">
        <div class="vrow"><span class="k">BMI</span><span class="n" id="b-bmi">–</span></div>
        <div class="vrow"><span class="k">Clearance vs. normal BMI</span><span class="n" id="b-cl">–</span></div>
        <div class="vrow"><span class="k">Levonorgestrel half-life</span><span class="n" id="b-lng">–</span></div>
        <div class="vrow"><span class="k">Norethindrone half-life (assumed)</span><span class="n" id="b-net">–</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="summary" id="b-say"></p>
    </div>
    <div class="explain">
      <div class="step"><h3>What was measured</h3><p>Edelman et al. (2009) gave Alesse to 10 women of normal BMI (mean 21.9) and 10 with obesity (mean 37.3). Levonorgestrel's half-life was 25.6 h vs. 52.1 h, with no difference in volume of distribution or SHBG. Same volume and twice the half-life means the body cleared it about half as fast.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>What the model does</h3><p>Clearance is normal up to BMI 21.9, falls smoothly to 49% at 37.3, and stays there above it (the heaviest women in the study looked like the rest of the obese group). Only the removal rate changes; absorption and volumes don't.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>What's assumed</h3><p>Only two groups were measured, so the in-between curve is a guess. <b>No one has measured this for norethindrone</b>; the minipill gets the same change as an untested assumption. The label studies are taken to be normal-BMI women.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="callout">What this model can and can't say: slower clearance means levels stay higher, so in the model a higher BMI makes a late pill dip <em>less</em> for both pills. Edelman's concern was different: obese women took about twice as long to reach steady levels at the start of each pack, and had a lower first-day peak (1.9 vs. 2.5 ng/mL, not statistically significant). Slower clearance alone can't produce a lower peak, so whatever caused it isn't in this model.</p>
